## Aliasing principle

Aliases are accepted when:
- The canonical page already covers the concept under a different name (verified by reading the page).
- {{OWNER_NAME}} uses both terms interchangeably in source material (not as a distinction).
- Forcing a new page would create redundancy, not clarity.

Aliases are NOT accepted when:
- The canonical page only mentions the concept in passing.
- {{OWNER_NAME}} treats the alias and the canonical as distinct concepts (then build a separate page — the "Never Merge Distinct Concepts" rule applies).
- The alias has its own downstream protocol implications.
